  "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =6+\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x>-1$ we have $(x+1)^{2}<(x+4)^{2},$ and thus $\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}>\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}<\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}$ is always true in the given domain, and we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is $(\\,-1,\\infty \\,).$",
